```java
private void processDegreeInput(EditText degreeText, SeekArc seekArc) {
    int previousDegree = degree;
    String degreeStr = degreeText.getText().toString().trim();

    if (degreeStr.isEmpty()) {
        degree = (degree == 0) ? (int) (seekArc.getProgress() * 3.6) : previousDegree;
    } else {
        try {
            degree = Integer.parseInt(degreeStr);
        } catch (NumberFormatException e) {
            degreeText.setText(getResources().getString(R.string.zero));
            seekArc.setProgress(0);
            toastInvalidValueMessage();
            return;
        }
    }

    if (degree > 360 || degree < 0) {
        degreeText.setText(getResources().getString(R.string.zero));
        seekArc.setProgress(0);
        toastInvalidValueMessage();
    } else {
        seekArc.setProgress((int) (degree / 3.6));
        editEnter = true;
    }
}
```

The issue is with how empty inputs are handled. When a user clears a text field, the code checks if `degree == 0` to decide whether to use the seek bar position or keep the previous value. But this logic is broken because the `degree` variable is shared across all servos, so after setting servo 1 to 90 degrees, the variable holds 90, and when the user clears servo 2's input, it incorrectly uses the seek bar position instead of preserving servo 2's previous value. The problem is that the shared `degree` variable gets overwritten when editing different servos, so when one servo clears its input, it incorrectly uses the seekArc value from whichever servo was last touched instead of its own previous value. The fix requires passing the EditText itself to `processDegreeInput` so each servo can restore its own prior degree, and then in `onOptionsItemSelected`, I need to update the case handlers to pass both the EditText and SeekArc instances to the method.

Actually, I'm reconsidering whether tracking each servo's previous degree separately is the right approach. Looking back at the bug description, it mentions a cached value being used incorrectly when the user enters a value for one servo and then interacts with another—this suggests the issue might be simpler than I thought, possibly just about which servo's previous value gets restored when clearing inputs.

Let me trace through the actual bug scenario: when the user enters a value for servo 1, then clears servo 2's input and hits enter, servo 2 incorrectly gets set to servo 1's value instead of remaining at its original value. This happens because the code reuses a shared `degree` variable across servo operations, causing the wrong previous value to be restored.

The real problem is that `degree` is a single shared field tracking the last modified servo's angle, so when clearing a different servo's input, it pulls that shared value instead of the target servo's own previous state. I need separate tracking for each servo's last degree value—`degree1`, `degree2`, `degree3`, `degree4`—so each servo restores its own correct previous value when cleared.

The fix involves adding individual fields for each servo's previous angle, initializing them to 0 when the activity starts, and updating them whenever a servo's value is set through the seek bar or text input.

